{# ============================================================================
   THE ROSTER'S WIDGET LIBRARY — the FIRST section of the module's configure
   page, exactly where every other module in the platform keeps it.

   IT IS NOT A DOOR ON THE DASHBOARD. Editing never happens on the surface
   being edited; the dashboard carries no customize control at all, and this
   screen is reached from the configure strip like Rotation, Watches and
   Settings.

   THE PAGE IS ONLY CHROME. Everything below the note is the shell's own
   preset component, identical on every surface that ships a catalogue and
   parameterised by this one's catalogue, partial name and routes. There are
   no roster-specific widget mechanics anywhere.

   AREA-SCOPED: every url the component is handed names this area, so
   arranging one area's roster leaves every other area's alone.
   ========================================================================== #}
{% extends '@UhifadhiRoster/configure/_frame.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Roster · widget library{% endblock %}

{# THE MAP SHEET IS THE PAGE'S TO LINK. This surface composes a widget that
   draws a plate, and the plate's styles do not travel with it the way the
   calendar's and the chart's do — a page that renders one and forgets this
   gets the map as a collapsed strip with its key inlined, and nothing
   errors. Leaflet's own sheet is not linked: the UX Map bridge imports it. #}
{% block stylesheets %}
    {{ parent() }}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset(constant('Uhifadhi\\Bundle\\AtlasBundle\\AtlasBundle::STYLESHEET')) }}">
{% endblock %}

{% block javascripts %}
    {{ parent() }}
    {# type="module" is deferred by definition, so the library is armed after
       the DOM exists. The script is the widget framework's — one library,
       every surface. #}
    <script type="module">
        import { initWidgetLibrary } from 'uhifadhi/widgets';
        initWidgetLibrary();
    </script>
{% endblock %}

{% block roster_trail_tail %} <a href="{{ path(constant('Uhifadhi\\Roster\\Controller\\RosterController::OVERVIEW_ROUTE'), {uuid: area.uuid}) }}">roster</a> / configure / widgets{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page_actions %}
    {# Throwing the whole composition away asks first — through the shell's
       reusable confirm modal, never the browser's own dialog. #}
    <button type="button" class="tgl w-act" {{ constant('Uhifadhi\\Bundle\\ShellBundle\\Widget\\Model\\WidgetDom::RESET') }}
            {{ stimulus_controller('confirm-modal') }}
            data-action="click->confirm-modal#ask"
            data-confirm-modal-title-value="Reset your widget layout?"
            data-confirm-modal-message-value="This area's roster dashboard goes back to the composition the module ships with. Your own designs are kept, and your other areas are untouched."
            data-confirm-modal-confirm-label-value="Reset layout"
            data-confirm-modal-danger-value="true">{{ ux_icon('shell:rotate-ccw') }}Reset to defaults</button>
{% endblock %}

{% block roster_section %}
    <div class="rfrag"><b>The directions are presets, not pages</b><em>the roster was drawn five ways &mdash; as a day board, station first, a week planner, the people, and right now. None became a screen of its own: each is a headed section below and a design that composes it. Adopt one, copy it, then pull a widget out of another into your copy.</em></div>

    {{ include('@Shell/widget/_library.html.twig', {
        catalog: catalog,
        builtins: builtins,
        customPresets: customPresets,
        active: active,
        widgets: widgets,
        partial: partial,
        widgetContext: widgetContext,
        urls: urls,
        csrfToken: csrfToken,
    }, with_context: false) }}

    {# THE SECOND SURFACE ON THIS PAGE. The Live tab's rail composes a
       COLUMN out of lists where the dashboard composes a page out of
       cards; they share the mechanism and nothing else, which is exactly
       what a surface is for. Both are declared here because this is the
       module's library, and a person arranging one will want the other.

       THE ANCHOR IS THE RAIL'S OWN DOOR: the Live tab links `#rail`. #}
    <h2 class="zone" id="rail">The Live tab&rsquo;s plate rail</h2>
    <p class="pgsub">The column beside <a href="{{ path(constant('Uhifadhi\\Roster\\Controller\\RosterController::LIVE_ROUTE'), {uuid: area.uuid}) }}">the Live tab</a>&rsquo;s plate is a widget surface of its own, and the lists it carries are widgets. Adopt one of the three arrangements this module ships, or compose your own &mdash; the rail is one column wide, so every widget in it is full width.</p>

    <div class="c">
        <span class="tab">{{ railCatalog.surface }}<span class="src">&middot; {{ railWidgets|length }} lists &middot; {{ railCatalog.presets|length }} arrangements</span></span>
        {% for preset in railCatalog.presets %}
            <div class="rset">
                <span class="lb"><b>{{ preset.label }}</b><em>{{ preset.layout|keys|join(' · ') }}</em></span>
                <span class="ct">
                    <a class="mchip{{ preset.id == railActive ? ' on' : '' }}"
                       href="{{ path(constant('Uhifadhi\\Roster\\Controller\\RosterController::RAIL_PRESET_ROUTE'), {uuid: area.uuid, presetId: preset.id}) }}">{{ preset.id == railActive ? 'in force' : 'adopt' }}</a>
                </span>
                <span class="why">{{ preset.description }}</span>
            </div>
        {% endfor %}

        {% for widget in railWidgets %}
            <div class="rfrag"><b>{{ widget.label }}</b><em>{{ widget.note }}</em></div>
        {% endfor %}
    </div>
{% endblock %}
